WEBVTT

00:00.720 --> 00:02.860
A function-- and I'm
going to speak about it

00:02.860 --> 00:06.350
in very abstract terms right
now-- is something that

00:06.350 --> 00:11.134
will take an input, and
it'll munch on that input,

00:11.134 --> 00:13.050
it'll look at that input,
it will do something

00:13.050 --> 00:14.010
to that input.

00:14.010 --> 00:20.230
And based on what that input is,
it will produce a given output.

00:20.230 --> 00:22.200
What is an example
of a function?

00:22.200 --> 00:27.940
I could have something
like f of x-- and x

00:27.940 --> 00:30.200
tends to be the variable
most used for an input

00:30.200 --> 00:31.200
into the function.

00:31.200 --> 00:34.100
And the name of a
function, f tends

00:34.100 --> 00:35.650
to be the most-used variable.

00:35.650 --> 00:41.680
But we'll see that you can
use others-- is equal to,

00:41.680 --> 00:48.360
let's say, x squared,
if x is even.

00:48.360 --> 00:59.680
And let's say it is equal
to x plus 5, if x is odd.

00:59.680 --> 01:03.960
What would happen if we
input 2 into this function?

01:03.960 --> 01:05.910
The way that we would
denote inputting 2

01:05.910 --> 01:10.730
is that we would want
to evaluate f of 2.

01:10.730 --> 01:14.760
This is saying, let's input
2 into our function f.

01:14.760 --> 01:16.660
And everywhere we
see this x here,

01:16.660 --> 01:19.320
this variable-- you can kind
of use as a placeholder-- let's

01:19.320 --> 01:21.390
replace it with our input.

01:21.390 --> 01:21.980
So let's see.

01:21.980 --> 01:24.810
If 2 is even, do 2 squared.

01:24.810 --> 01:27.870
If 2 is odd, do 2 plus 5.

01:27.870 --> 01:31.670
Well, 2 is even, so we're
going to do 2 squared.

01:31.670 --> 01:36.250
In this case, f of 2 is
going to be 2 squared, or 4.

01:36.250 --> 01:40.540
Now what would f of 3 be?

01:40.540 --> 01:42.640
Well, once again, everywhere
we see this variable,

01:42.640 --> 01:44.135
we'll replace it with our input.

01:48.250 --> 01:54.950
So f of 3, 3 squared if 3 is
even, 3 plus 5 if 3 is odd.

01:54.950 --> 01:57.390
Well, 3 is odd, so it's
going to be 3 plus 5.

01:57.390 --> 02:02.670
It is going to be equal to 8.

02:02.670 --> 02:04.200
You might say, OK,
that's neat, Sal.

02:04.200 --> 02:05.790
This was kind of
an interesting way

02:05.790 --> 02:09.139
to define a function, a way to
kind of munch on these numbers.

02:09.139 --> 02:12.217
But I could have done this
with traditional equations

02:12.217 --> 02:13.800
in some way, especially
if you allowed

02:13.800 --> 02:16.140
me to use the squirrelly
bracket thing.

02:16.140 --> 02:19.710
What can a function do that
maybe my traditional toolkits

02:19.710 --> 02:22.160
might have not been
as expressive about?

02:22.160 --> 02:25.880
Well, you could even do
a function like this.

02:25.880 --> 02:27.790
Let me not use f
and x anymore, just

02:27.790 --> 02:30.800
to show you that the notation
is more general than that.

02:30.800 --> 02:47.050
I could say h of a is equal to
the next largest number that

02:47.050 --> 02:57.886
starts with the same
letter as variable a.

03:00.460 --> 03:03.330
And we're going to assume
that we're dealing in English.

03:03.330 --> 03:09.930
Given that, what is
h of 2 going to be?

03:09.930 --> 03:12.890
Well, 2 starts with a T.
What's the next largest

03:12.890 --> 03:14.210
number that starts with a T?

03:14.210 --> 03:18.610
Well, it's going
to be equal to 3.

03:18.610 --> 03:25.210
Now what would h of-- I don't
know, let's think about this,

03:25.210 --> 03:27.990
h of 8 be equal to?

03:27.990 --> 03:31.570
Well, 8 starts with an E.
The next largest number

03:31.570 --> 03:37.400
that starts with an E-- it's
not 9, 10-- it would be 11.

03:37.400 --> 03:42.100
And so now you see it's a
very, very, very general tool.

03:42.100 --> 03:44.630
This h function that we just
defined, we'll look at it.

03:44.630 --> 03:46.500
We'll look at the
letter that the number

03:46.500 --> 03:47.869
starts with in English.

03:47.869 --> 03:49.910
So it's doing this really,
really, really, really

03:49.910 --> 03:50.990
wacky thing.

03:50.990 --> 03:53.070
Now not all functions
have to be this wacky.

03:53.070 --> 03:55.950
In fact, you have already
been dealing with functions.

03:55.950 --> 04:02.340
You have seen things like
y is equal to x plus 1.

04:02.340 --> 04:04.080
This can be viewed
as a function.

04:04.080 --> 04:07.520
We could write this
as y is a function

04:07.520 --> 04:11.760
of x, which is
equal to x plus 1.

04:11.760 --> 04:16.390
If you give as an
input-- let me write

04:16.390 --> 04:23.650
it this way-- for example,
when x is 0 we could say f of 0

04:23.650 --> 04:25.710
is equal to, well, you take 0.

04:25.710 --> 04:26.830
You add 1.

04:26.830 --> 04:28.440
It's equal to 1.

04:28.440 --> 04:30.447
f of 2 is equal to 2.

04:30.447 --> 04:31.780
You've already done this before.

04:31.780 --> 04:33.760
You've done things
where you said, look,

04:33.760 --> 04:37.250
let me make a table of
x and put our y's there.

04:37.250 --> 04:40.230
When x is 0, y is 1.

04:40.230 --> 04:42.990
I'm sorry.

04:42.990 --> 04:44.150
I made a little mistake.

04:44.150 --> 04:46.660
Where f of 2 is equal to 3.

04:46.660 --> 04:48.920
And you've done this before
with tables where you say,

04:48.920 --> 04:51.470
look, x and y.

04:51.470 --> 04:53.650
When x is 0, y is 1.

04:53.650 --> 04:57.151
When x is 2, y is 3.

04:57.151 --> 04:59.400
You might say, well, what
was the whole point of using

04:59.400 --> 05:01.310
the function
notation here to say

05:01.310 --> 05:03.820
f of x is equal to x plus 1?

05:03.820 --> 05:07.390
The whole point is to think
in these more general terms.

05:07.390 --> 05:09.140
For something like
this, you didn't really

05:09.140 --> 05:11.417
have to introduce
function notations.

05:11.417 --> 05:14.000
But it doesn't hurt to introduce
function notations because it

05:14.000 --> 05:17.820
makes it very clear that
the function takes an input,

05:17.820 --> 05:21.520
takes my x-- in this
definition it munches on it.

05:21.520 --> 05:23.500
It says, OK, x plus 1.

05:23.500 --> 05:26.080
And then it produces
1 more than it.

05:26.080 --> 05:29.460
So here, whatever the input
is, the output is 1 more

05:29.460 --> 05:31.100
than that original function.

05:31.100 --> 05:32.350
Now I know what you're asking.

05:32.350 --> 05:32.850
All right.

05:32.850 --> 05:35.210
Well, what is not
a function then?

05:35.210 --> 05:36.750
Well, remember,
we said a function

05:36.750 --> 05:39.850
is something that takes an
input and produces only one

05:39.850 --> 05:43.130
possible output for
that given input.

05:43.130 --> 05:46.580
For example-- and let
me look at a visual way

05:46.580 --> 05:49.100
of thinking about a function
this time, or a relationship,

05:49.100 --> 05:53.190
I should say-- let's
say that's our y-axis,

05:53.190 --> 05:55.463
and this right over
here is our x-axis.

05:58.241 --> 06:05.720
Let me draw a circle
here that has radius 2.

06:05.720 --> 06:07.950
So it's a circle of radius 2.

06:07.950 --> 06:09.300
This is negative 2.

06:09.300 --> 06:11.120
This is positive 2.

06:11.120 --> 06:12.730
This is negative 2.

06:12.730 --> 06:15.940
So my circle, it's
centered at the origin.

06:15.940 --> 06:17.330
It has radius 2.

06:17.330 --> 06:20.560
That's my best attempt
at drawing the circle.

06:20.560 --> 06:21.920
Let me fill it in.

06:21.920 --> 06:23.100
So this is a circle.

06:23.100 --> 06:25.070
The equation of
this circle is going

06:25.070 --> 06:28.370
to be x squared plus y squared
is equal to the radius squared,

06:28.370 --> 06:31.360
is equal to 2 squared,
or it's equal to 4.

06:31.360 --> 06:34.027
The question is, is this
relationship between x and y--

06:34.027 --> 06:35.610
here I've expressed
it as an equation.

06:35.610 --> 06:40.020
Here I've visually drawn all
of the x's and y's that satisfy

06:40.020 --> 06:43.220
this equation-- is this
relationship between x

06:43.220 --> 06:45.280
and y a function?

06:45.280 --> 06:47.060
And we can see
visually that it's not

06:47.060 --> 06:48.280
going to be a function.

06:48.280 --> 06:49.850
You pick a given x.

06:49.850 --> 06:53.820
Let's say x is equal to 1.

06:53.820 --> 06:57.150
There's two possible y's
that are associated with it,

06:57.150 --> 07:00.237
this y up here and
this y down here.

07:00.237 --> 07:02.570
We could even solve for that
by looking at the equation.

07:02.570 --> 07:06.210
When x is equal to 1, we
get 1 squared plus y squared

07:06.210 --> 07:07.073
is equal to 4.

07:07.073 --> 07:11.390
1 plus y squared is equal to 4.

07:11.390 --> 07:15.720
Or subtracting 1 from both
sides, y squared is equal to 3.

07:15.720 --> 07:20.830
Or y is equal to the positive or
the negative square root of 3.

07:20.830 --> 07:23.720
This right over here is the
positive square root of 3,

07:23.720 --> 07:26.700
and this right over here is
the negative square root of 3.

07:26.700 --> 07:28.810
So this situation,
this relationship

07:28.810 --> 07:34.130
where I inputted a 1
into my little box here,

07:34.130 --> 07:36.680
and associated with
the 1, I associate

07:36.680 --> 07:40.510
both a positive square root of
3 and a negative square root

07:40.510 --> 07:47.000
of 3, this is not a function.

07:47.000 --> 07:51.540
I cannot associate with my
input two different outputs.

07:51.540 --> 07:56.240
I can only have one
output for a given input.
